1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isabella of Angoulême (French : Isabelle d'Angoulême; 1188 - 31 May 1246) was Countess of Angoulême  and queen consort of England .  Queen of England She was the only daughter and heir
2 CONC  of Aymer Taillefer , Count of Angoulême , by Alice de Courtenay . Her paternal grandparents were William IV of Angoulême , Count of Angouleme and Marguerite de Turenne. Her maternal grandparents were
2 CONC  Pierre de Courtenay  and Elizabeth de Courtenay. Her maternal great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France . She became Countess of Angoulême  in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already
2 CONC  queen of England. Her marriage to King John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux , a year after he annulled  his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester . Isabella was originally betrothed to Hug
2 CONC h le Brun, Count of Lusignan, son of the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all of their French lands, and a
2 CONC rmed conflict ensued. At the time of her marriage to John, the 12-year-old Isabella was already renowned for her beauty and has sometimes been called the Helen  of the Middle Ages  by historians. Howe
2 CONC ver, her marriage to John cannot be said to have been successful, in part because she was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Second marriage When John died in 1216, 
2 CONC Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France  and in 1220, proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan  Count of La Marche . It is unclear whether it had been Hugh X or his father to whom Isab
2 CONC ella had been betrothed before her marriage to King John. By Hugh X, Isabella had nine more children. Their eldest son Hugh XI of Lusignan  succeeded his father as Count of La Marche  and Count of Ang
2 CONC ouleme  in 1249. Death and burial Isabella was accused of plotting against King Louis IX of France  in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey , where she died on 31 May 1246, and was buried there. At her
2 CONC  own insistence, she was first buried in the churchyard, as an act of repentance for her many misdeeds. On a visit to Fontevrault, her son King Henry III of England  was shocked to find her buried out
2 CONC side the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside. She was finally placed beside Henry II  and Eleanor of Aquitaine . Afterwards, most of her many children, having few prospects in France, set s
2 CONC ail for England and the court of Henry, their half-brother.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (Fr. Isabelle d'Angoulême ; c. 1187 – May 31, 1246) was countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England. She was the only daughter and heir of Aymer Taillifer, Count of Angoul
2 CONC ême, by Alix de Courtenay; her maternal great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her ma
2 CONC rriage to King John took place on August 24, 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marriage. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged about thirteen, and her beauty was renowned;
2 CONC  she is sometimes called the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians. It could not be said to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character 
2 CONC to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh X of Lusignan[1], son of the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip I
2 CONC I of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed conflict ensued. When John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France and in 1220 proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lus
2 CONC ignan, now Count of La Marche, her former fiancé. Isabella was accused of plotting against the French king in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey, where she died on May 31, 1246, and was buried there.
2 CONC  Afterwards most of her many children, having few prospects in France, set sail for England and the court of their half-brother King Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT With King John of England: 5 children, all of whom survived into adulthood, including: King Henry III of England (b. 1207 – d. 1272) Richard, Earl of Cornwall and King of the Romans (b. 1209 – d. 1272
2 CONC ) Joan (b. 1210 – d. 1238), the wife of King Alexander II of Scotland Isabella (b. 1214 – d. 1241), the wife of Emperor Frederick II Eleanor (b. 1215 – d. 1275), who would marry William Marshal, 2nd E
2 CONC arl of Pembroke
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabelle went into hiding (inside the abbey) due to her being blamed for the death of her husband, John. She remained in hideing (in a secret chamber) until her death. She was buried, upon her request
2 CONC , in the open cemetery (common graveyard) at Fontevrault. Some years later her son, Henry III, moved her body into the choir of the Abbey Church & commissioned the fine effigy which is the only near c
2 CONC ontemporary likeness of her.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (French: Isabelle d'Angoulême) was Countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already qu
2 CONC een of England. Her marriage to King John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged
2 CONC  about twelve, and her beauty was renowned; she is sometimes called the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT It could not be said to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh le 
2 CONC Brun, Count of Lusignan, son of the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed confl
2 CONC ict ensued. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She had five children with King John, including our ancestor King Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France and in 1220 proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, now Count of La Marche, her former fiancé. By him, Isabella had ni
2 CONC ne more children, including our ancestor Alice of Lusignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was accused of plotting against King Louis IX of France in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey, where she died on 31 May 1246, and was buried there. At her own insistence she was first buried
2 CONC  in the churchyard, as an act of repentance for her many misdeeds. On a visit to Fontevrault her son King Henry III of England was shocked to find her buried outside the Abbey and ordered her immediat
2 CONC ely moved inside.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was our ancestor through two distinct descent lines--through her son King Henry III and through her daughter Alice of Lusignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Angoul%C3%AAme for more information.
