2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angouleme married John Lackland of England, son of Eleanor of Aquitaine and Henry II of England. John had put aside his first wife in 1198. Isabella was twelve years old at her marriage to
2 CONC  John.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1202, Isabella's father died, and she became Countess of Angouleme in her own right.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage of Isabella and John was not an easy one. John was infatuated with his young and beautiful wife, but they both were reported to have engaged in adultery, and to have had strong tempers wh
2 CONC ich they used on each other. When John suspected Isabella of having had an affair, he had her suspected lover hanged and then dangled above her bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella and John had five children before John died in 1216. At John's death, Isabella's quick action had her son Henry crowned at John's death, in Gloucester where they were at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Isabella had 5 children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT # King Henry III (b. October 1, 1207)
2 CONT # Richard, Earl of Cornwall, King of the Romans
2 CONT # Joan (married Alexander II of Scotland)
2 CONT # Isabella (married Emperor Frederick II)
2 CONT # Eleanor (married William Marshall and then Simon de Montfort)
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Epilogue'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT After John's death Isabella went on to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche.  They had many scandalous adventures together, some of which you may read for yourself if you are so inclined.
2 CONT
2 CONT *By her own prior arrangement, Isabella was first buried in the Fontevraud Abbey's churchyard, as an act of repentance for her many misdeeds.  On a visit to Fontevraud, her son King Henry III of Engla
2 CONC nd was shocked to find her buried outside the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside. She was finally placed beside Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (French: Isabelle d'Angoulême, IPA: [izabɛl dɑ̃ɡulɛm]; 1188[1] – 31 May 1246) was suo jure Countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England as the second wife of King John. She
2 CONC  was queen from 24 August 1200 until John's death on 19 October 1216. She had five children by the king including his heir Henry  who succeeded John as Henry III of England. In 1220, Isabella married 
2 CONC secondly the man to whom she had been originally betrothed, Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche, by whom she had another nine children. Hugh had been promised to her eldest daughter, Joan, but the 
2 CONC latter was instead married to King Alexander II of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1241, Isabella formed a conspiracy against King Louis IX of France, after being publicly snubbed by his mother, Blanche of Castile for whom she had a deep-seated hatred.[2] In 1244, after the plot 
2 CONC had failed, Isabella was accused of attempting to poison the king, and to avoid arrest, sought refuge in Fontevraud Abbey where she died two years later at the age of about 58.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Angoul%C3%AAme
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella became Countess of Angoulême in her own right on 16 June 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage to King John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux, a year afte
2 CONC r he annulled his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester. She was crowned queen in an elaborate ceremony on 9 October at Westminster Abbey in London. Isabella was originally betrothed to Hugh IX le Br
2 CONC un, Count of Lusignan,[2] son of the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all of their French lands, and armed
2 CONC  conflict ensued.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of her marriage to John, the 12-year-old Isabella was already renowned for her beauty[3] and has sometimes been called the Helen of the Middle Ages by historians.[4] However, her marriage 
2 CONC to John cannot be said to have been successful, in part because she was much younger than her husband and possessed a volatile temper to match his own. King John, however, was deeply infatuated with h
2 CONC is young, beautiful wife; he neglected his state affairs to spend time with Isabella, often remaining in bed with her until noon, although it was the custom for kings to rise at five o'clock in the mo
2 CONC rning to commence their duties. The common people began to term her a "siren" or "Messalina", although they were pleased with her beauty.[5] Her mother-in-law, Eleanor of Aquitaine readily accepted he
2 CONC r as John's wife.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 October 1207 at Winchester Castle, Isabella gave birth to a son and heir who was named Henry after the King's father, Henry II. He was quickly followed by another son, Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwa
2 CONC ll, King of the Romans; and three daughters, Joan, Isabel, and Eleanor. All five children survived into adulthood, and would make illustrious marriages; all but Joan would produce offspring of their o
2 CONC wn.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angouleme Queen of England was born circa 1187. She married John "Lackland" Plantagenet King of England, son of Henry II "Curtmantle" Plantagenet King of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine Q
2 CONC ueen of England, on 24 August 1200 at Bordeaux Cathedral, Bordeaux, Gironde, France. As of 24 August 1200,her married name was Plantagenet. Isabella of Angouleme Queen of England died on 31 May 1246 a
2 CONC t Fontevraud, Maine-et-Loire, France.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Only daughter and heir of Aymer, Comte d'Angouleme. Married secondly to Hugh le Brun, Comte de la Marche, in Poitou, France, by who she had issue William de Lusignan, otherwise de Valnce, from whom de
2 CONC scended the line of Valence, Earls of Pembroke. From A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, by Sir Bernard Burke, new edn., publ. 1866 (
2 CONC London: Harrison), p. 545, and from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Angoul%C3%AAme.
